,158,120,77\n78,2012-07-12,66,50.8,2.2\n79,2012-07-19,77,62,1.6\n80,2012-09-11,62,45,4.3\n81,2012-10-24,62,38.2,4.7\n82,2012-11-14,19,11,6.6\n83,2012-12-12,9,7.9,5.4\n84,2013-01-16,8,7.7,6.6\n85,2013-01-29,32,18.9,7.9\n86,2013-02-13,7,5,7.9\n87,2013-03-12,44,20,22\n88,2013-03-13,37,20,53.8\n89,2013-03-27,13,9.32,10\n90,2013-04-15,48,32,39\n91,2013-04-15,63,32,39\n92,2013-04-24,119,105,82\n93,2013-05-06,132,85,101\n94,2013-05-09,463,367,114\n95,2013-05-15,140,85.5,32\n96,2013-05-21,192,120,50\n97,2013-05-28,109,61.3,15.2\n98,2013-06-13,120,61,30.7\n99,2013-06-24,154,69.7,22.7\n100,2013-07-09,86,48.4,3.3\n101,2013-07-29,215,160,2390\n102,2013-07-29,236,160,2370\n103,2013-08-07,154,85.5,13800\n104,2013-09-12,28,17,65.3\n105,2013-10-24,14,7.63,30\n106,2013-11-25,6,3.4,37.3\n107,2014-01-14,9,4.13,45\n108,2014-02-20,18,8.13,49\n109,2014-03-17,13,6.6,37\n110,2014-04-09,22,11,30.5\n111,2014-05-08,46,20,22\n112,2014-06-03,58,34,50.8\n113,2014-06-05,130,69,141\n114,2014-06-09,285,180,1470\n115,2014-06-12,441,220,3880\n116,2014-06-24,157,90,278\n117,2014-07-10,57,37.3,88.3\n118,2014-07-15,59,32.3,46\n119,2014-07-24,52,29.8,28\n120,2014-08-04,62,27.5,21.2\n"},{"name":"usgs_07144100_sediment_2004_2014.json","mediaType":"application/json","bytes":3165,"sha":"748cb4ef126b346d001fe86211ee6b2a2632712e36889862056c8a28036cd5ff","text":"{\n  \"schema\": \"upstate.public-sediment-reference/v1\",\n  \"title\": \"USGS Little Arkansas River near Sedgwick calibration table, 2004–2014\",\n  \"source_url\": \"https://water.usgs.gov/admin/memo/SW/sw.2016.07%2Bwq.2016.10.pdf\",\n  \"source_document\": \"USGS Office of Surface Water Technical Memorandum 2016.07 / Office of Water Quality Technical Memorandum 2016.10\",\n  \"source_document_date\": \"2016-09-30\",\n  \"source_location\": \"Attachment B, model archive summary; 120-row calibration table, printed pages 24–25 (PDF pages 24–25 when counted from 1)\",\n  \"station\": \"07144100\",\n  \"station_name\": \"Little Arkansas River near Sedgwick, Kansas\",\n  \"model_id\": \"07144100.SSC.WY07.1\",\n  \"sensor\": {\n    \"id\": \"YSI 6600 / 6136\",\n    \"method\": \"Optical turbidity, YSI model 6136\",\n    \"unit\": \"FNU\"\n  },\n  \"analyte\": \"SSC\",\n  \"response_unit\": \"mg/L\",\n  \"discharge_unit\": \"ft3/s\",\n  \"discharge_conversion\": {\n    \"to\": \"m3/s\",\n    \"factor\": 0.028316846592,\n    \"definition\": \"1 international foot = 0.3048 metres; cubic conversion is exact\"\n  },\n  \"date_precision\": \"date only; no clock time or timezone is supplied by the published table\",\n  \"pairing\": \"Each numbered published table row supplies concurrent SSC, turbidity and discharge. Original row numbers identify distinct samples, including repeated dates. A shared calendar date does not combine samples or establish independent hydrologic events.\",\n  \"transcription\": {\n    \"method\": \"Extract published table text, select original raw SSC/TURB/Q columns, preserve printed decimal values and row order; discard no rows. No published rounded logarithms used for fitting.\",\n    \"rows\": 120,\n    \"distinct_dates\": 115,\n    \"range\": {\n      \"start\": \"2004-07-27\",\n      \"end\": \"2014-08-04\"\n    },\n    \"duplicate_source_rows_checked\": true,\n    \"conflicts\": 0,\n    \"limitations\": \"These are values at the precision printed in the USGS model archive summary, not the original laboratory or sensor CSV byte stream. Original author removed seven samples before this published120-row table; their complete original values are not contained here.\"\n  },\n  \"published_model\": {\n    \"equation\": \"log10(SSC mg/L) = 0.262 + 0.933 log10(turbidity FNU) + 0.0431 log10(discharge ft3/s)\",\n    \"smearing_factor\": 1.02,\n    \"retransformed_equation\": \"SSC = 1.865 * turbidity^0.933 * discharge_ft3_s^0.0431\",\n    \"comparison\": \"Published coefficients are rounded. Re-fitting uses the raw columns at published precision; convert discharge units before comparing the intercept.\"\n  },\n  \"course\": {\n    \"full_calibration\": \"All120 published samples assigned to training to reproduce the archive coefficients; this is a calibration reproduction.\",\n    \"temporal_validation\": \"Separate study: dates before2012-01-01 assigned to training; dates from2012-01-01 assigned to validation. This is a new explicitly declared temporal holdout exercise, not the author's published cross-validation or independent new monitoring data.\"\n  },\n  \"license\": \"United States Geological Survey federal publication; source attribution retained.\",\n  \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-10-13\",\n  \"csv_sha256\": \"7783717d890ffc902096823a626b080cbaa457bc82977ebe97961abc74881e26\"\n}\n"},{"name":"usgs_07144100_sediment_table_extraction.txt","mediaType":"text/plain","bytes":34708,"sha":"42f9679306a429c6df3f3fe62922bb8bfd47bb556fa0f203b5b912c6203192fa","text":"Surrogate memo v2.doc.docx (https://water.usgs.gov/admin/memo/SW/sw.2016.07%2Bwq.2016.10.pdf)\n【turn308view0】 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 40; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn296view1\",\"lineno\":425}); Total lines: 1021\nL401@P11: the extrapolation range, then predicted values must either be:\nL402@P11: o censored at greater than the predicted value if the validation sample confirms\nL403@P11: that the direction of the model bias is indeed positive; for example, the model\nL404@P11: prediction is 100 but the validation sample is 200 then the predicted values\nL405@P11-12: may be set to >100, or13\nL406@P12: o removed or blocked in NWIS using thresholds if the validation sample does\nL407@P12: not confirm the direction of the model bias; for example, the model prediction\nL408@P12: is 100 but validation sample is 50.\nL409@P12: • Approved models may not be used for extrapolating more than 20 percent outside the\nL410@P12: range of the sample data used to fit the model until additional data are collected in that\nL411@P12: range and the model performance and sensor performance are validated or the model is\nL412@P12: refit using the new data. A minimum of one (but at least two are recommended)\nL413@P12: independent samples must be collected outside the range of the existing data to confirm\nL414@P12: the model is performing adequately. Model predictions made outside of the two previously\nL415@P12: mentioned allowable areas of extrapolation may not be served in the real-time water\nL416@P12: quality system and they must be removed or blocked in NWIS until the existing model is\nL417@P12: validated with the new data or a new model is fit that includes data in the range. Once a\nL418@P12: new model is approved, it may be used to make predictions for any data previously\nL419@P12: blocked in NWIS.\nL420@P12: • Approved models shall not be used for interpolating between time intervals for which the\nL421@P12: surrogate data are collected. That is, if surrogate data are collected at 30-minute intervals,\nL422@P12: the models may not be used to estimate at 15-minute intervals by averaging or otherwise\nL423@P12: interpolating between values of the surrogates collected at the longer time step.\nL424@P12: 9. Approved models can be disseminated and archived on the NRTWQ Web site\nL425@P12: (http://nrtwq.usgs.gov/). These time series are displayed in plots, tables, statistical\nL426@P12: summaries, and duration curves. Site and model information are also displayed. These\nL427@P12: computed values also can be displayed using NWIS, or using another approved data release\nL428@P12: method. As new models are employed, older models are archived along with the computed\nL429@P12: data. Models are numbered sequentially and include the station number, constituent, year the\nL430@P12: model was approved, and model version number if needed (for example,\nL431@P12: 06892350.SSC.WY15.ver1).\nL432@P12: 10. Surrogate turbidity and streamflow data for computed concentrations and loads are\nL433@P12: considered category 1 data and are approved by following the Continuous Records\nL434@P12: Processing (CRP) policy of the Water Mission Area (WRD Policy Numbered Memorandum\nL435@P12: 2010.02). Methods of collecting the surrogate measurements are documented in formal\nL436@P12: publications series such as USGS Techniques and Methods or the National Field Manual.\nL437@P12: Computed concentration and loads are approved following CRP policy under category 3\nL438@P12: which indicates approval to be completed within a year in most cases. All surrogate data\nL439@P12: must be stored in NWIS.\nL440@P12: 11. Circumstances may arise in which historical data need revision. For example, if errors are\nL441@P12-13: discovered in explanatory data such as turbidity, then errors also exist in computed 14\nL442@P13: suspended sediment data. Until further guidance is provided, Centers must use good\nL443@P13: judgment in deciding which circumstances justify correction of historical data.\nL444@P13: References\nL445@P13: Babyak, M.A., 2004, What you see may not be what you get-A brief, nontechnical introduction\nL446@P13: to overfitting in regression-type models: Psychosomatic Medicine, v. 66, no. 3, p. 411-421.\nL447@P13: Edwards, T.K., and Glysson, G.D., 1999, Field methods for measurement of fluvial sediment:\nL448@P13: U.S. Geological Survey Techniques of Water-Resources Investigations, book 3, chap. C2, 89\nL449@P13: p., accessed March 7, 2016, at https://pubs.er.usgs.gov/publication/twri03C2.\nL450@P13: Green, S.B., 1991, How many subjects does it take to do a regression analysis: Multivariate\nL451@P13: Behavioral Research, v. 26, no. 3, p.499-510.\nL452@P13: Harrell, F.E., Jr., 2001, Regression Modeling Strategies-With Applications to Linear Models,\nL453@P13: Logistic Regression, and Survival Analysis: Springer, New York, 568 p.\nL454@P13: Hipel, K.W., and McLeod, A.I., 1994, Modelling of Water Resources and Environmental\nL455@P13: Systems: Amsterdam, Elsevier, 1013 p.\nL456@P13: Landers, M.N., Straub, T.D., Wood, M.S., and Domanski, M.M., 2016, Sediment acoustic index\nL457@P13: method for computing continuous suspended-sediment concentrations: U.S. Geological Survey\nL458@P13: Techniques and Methods, book 3, chap. C5\nL459@P13: Rasmussen, P.P., Gray, J.R., Glysson, G.D., and Ziegler, A.C., 2009, Guidelines and procedures\nL460@P13: for computing time-series suspended-sediment concentrations and loads from in-stream\nL461@P13: turbidity-sensor and streamflow data: U.S. Geological Survey Techniques and Methods book\nL462@P13: 3, chap. C4, 53 p.\nL463@P13: U.S. Geological Survey, 2006, Collection of water samples (ver. 2.0): U.S. Geological Survey\nL464@P13: Techniques of Water-Resources Investigations, book 9, chap. A4, September 2006, accessed\nL465@P13: June 25, 2016, at http://pubs.water.usgs.gov/twri9A4/\nL466@P13: WRD Policy Numbered Memorandum No. 2010.02, Continuous Records Processing of all Water\nL467@P13: Time Series Data, accessed July, 2016 at\nL468@P13-14: http://water.usgs.gov/admin/memo/policy/wrdpolicy10.02.html15\nL469@P14: Attachment B. Example Model Archive Summary for a Turbidity Suspended-Sediment Model\nL470@P14: Model Archive Summary for Suspended-Sediment Concentration at Station 07144100;\nL471@P14: Little Arkansas River near Sedgwick, Kansas\nL472@P14: This model archive summary summarizes the suspended-sediment concentration (SSC) model developed to\nL473@P14: compute hourly SSC from January 1, 2007 onward. This model supersedes all models used from 2007 onward. The\nL474@P14: methods used follow USGS guidance as referenced in relevant Office of Surface Water/Office of Water Quality\nL475@P14: Technical Memoranda and USGS Techniques and Methods, book 3, chap. C4 (Rasmussen and others, 2009).\nL476@P14: Site and Model Information\nL477@P14: Site number: 07144100\nL478@P14: Site name: Little Arkansas River near Sedgwick, Kansas\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSurrogate memo v2.doc.docx (https://water.usgs.gov/admin/memo/SW/sw.2016.07%2Bwq.2016.10.pdf)\n【turn308view1】 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 40; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn296view1\",\"lineno\":500}); Total lines: 1021\nL479@P14: Location: Latitude 37°52'59\", longitude 97°25'27\" referenced to North American Datum of 1927, in NE ¼ NW ¼\nL480@P14: NW ¼ sec. 15, T. 25 S., R. 1 W., Sedgwick County, Kansas, Hydrologic Unit 11030012, on left bank at downstream\nL481@P14: side from county highway bridge, 2.1 miles (mi) south of Sedgwick, and at mile marker 23.7.\nL482@P14: Equipment: A YSI 6600 water-quality monitor equipped with sensors for water temperature, specific conductance\nL483@P14: (SC), dissolved oxygen, and pH, and a YSI Model 6136 turbidity sensor. The monitor is housed in a 4-inch plastic\nL484@P14: pipe and typically is placed in the deepest section of the river that has velocity similar to rest of the stream.\nL485@P14: Readings from the YSI 6600 are recorded every 30 minutes and transmitted by way of satellite, hourly. A YSI Model\nL486@P14: 6136 turbidity sensor started operation July 27, 2004.\nL487@P14: Model number: 07144100.SSC.WY07.1\nL488@P14: Date model was created: December 26, 2014\nL489@P14: Model calibration data period: July, 27 2004 to August 4, 2014\nL490@P14: Model application date: January 1, 2007 onward\nL491@P14: Computed by: Patrick Eslick, KS WSC, June 21, 2016\nL492@P14: Reviewed by: Patrick Rasmussen, KS WSC and Brian Kelly, KS WSC, July 8, 2016\nL493@P14: Approved by: Andy Ziegler, KS WSC Director, July 20, 2016\nL494@P14: Model Data\nL495@P14: All data were collected using U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) protocols and are stored in the National Water\nL496@P14: Information System (NWIS) database (http://dx.doi.org/10.5066/F7P55KJN). The regression model is based on\nL497@P14: 120 concurrent measurements of suspended-sediment concentration, streamflow, and turbidity samples collected\nL498@P14: from July 27, 2004 through August 4, 2014. Samples were collected throughout the range of continuously observed\nL499@P14: hydrologic and turbidity conditions. Summary statistics and the complete model-calibration data are provided in\nL500@P14: the dataset. Studentized residuals from the final model were inspected for values greater than 3 or less than\nL501@P14: negative 3. Values outside of the 3 to -3 range are considered potential outliers and were investigated. Samples\nL502@P14: collected May 29, 2008; December 1, 2009; December 17, 2009; and December 11, 2013; June 13, 2010; July 6,\nL503@P14-15: 2010; and August 15, 2013 were deemed outliers and were removed from the dataset. 16\nL504@P15: Sediment Data\nL505@P15: Cross-section samples are collected either from the downstream side of the bridge or instream upstream near the\nL506@P15: bridge. The equal-width-increment or multi-vertical method is used, and samples typically are composited for\nL507@P15: analysis. Cross-section samples are obtained during all discrete sample collections every month and during\nL508@P15: selected runoff events. A FISP US D-95 with a Teflon bottle, cap, and nozzle depth-integrating sampler is used\nL509@P15: from the bridge; and a DH-81with a Teflon bottle, cap, and nozzle hand sampler or a grab sample with a Teflon\nL510@P15: bottle is used for wading samples. Samples are analyzed for SSC and/ or LOI and 5-point grain size in the USGS\nL511@P15: Sediment Laboratory in Iowa City, Iowa.\nL512@P15: Surrogate Data\nL513@P15: The turbidity data used in this analysis were measured using a YSI model 6136 installed and in use from 2007\nL514@P15: onward. The 6136 replaced the old YSI model 6026 sensor. Data from the YSI 6026 and 6136 sensors are not\nL515@P15: equivalent and therefore cannot be used interchangeably with this new SSC model.\nL516@P15: See the Station Analysis for the streamflow and turbidity records for more information (available upon request).\nL517@P15: Model Development\nL518@P15: Regression analysis was done using R by examining turbidity and other continuously measured data as\nL519@P15: explanatory variables for estimating suspended-sediment concentration. A variety of models that predict SSC and\nL520@P15: models that predict log10(SSC) were evaluated. The distribution of residuals was examined for normality and plots\nL521@P15: of residuals (the difference between the measured and predicted values) as compared to predicted SSC were\nL522@P15: examined for homoscedasticity (meaning that their departures from zero did not change substantially over the\nL523@P15: range of predicted values). This comparison of several models led to the conclusion that the most appropriate and\nL524@P15: reliable model would be one that estimated log10(SSC).\nL525@P15: Turbidity and streamflow were selected as the best predictors of log10(SSC) based on residual plots, relatively high\nL526@P15: adjusted coefficient of determination (adjusted R2), and relatively low model standard percentage error (MSPE).\nL527@P15: Values for all of the aforementioned statistics and metrics were computed and are included below along with all\nL528@P15: relevant sample data and more in-depth statistical information.\nL529@P15: Model Summary\nL530@P15: Summary of final regression analysis for suspended-sediment concentration at site number 07144100.\nL531@P15: Suspended-sediment concentration-based model:\nL532@P15: log10(𝑆𝑆𝑆) = 0.933 × log10(𝑇𝑇𝑇𝑇) + 0.0431 × log10(𝑄) + 0.262 ,\nL533@P15: where\nL534@P15: SSC = suspended-sediment concentration, in milligrams per liter (mg/L);\nL535@P15: Q = streamflow in cubic feet per second (ft3/s); and,\nL536@P15: TURB = Turbidity, YSI model 6136, in formazin nephelometric units (FNU).\nL537@P15: The use of turbidity and streamflow as explanatory variables is appropriate physically and statistically. Turbidity\nL538@P15: makes sense physically because suspended sediment is composed of particles that scatter light in water.\nL539@P15: Suspended sediment correlates well with streamflow because high streamflow values tend to increase\nL540@P15: concentrations of SSC. The relation between turbidity and SSC can vary given varying concentrations of organic\nL541@P15: suspended particles that increase turbidity, but are not included in the SSC analysis.\nL542@P15: The log-transformed model may be retransformed to the original units so that SSC can be calculated directly. The\nL543@P15: retransformation introduces a bias in the calculated constituent. This bias may be corrected using Duan’s Bias\nL544@P15: Correction Factor (BCF). For this model, the calculated BCF is 1.02. The retransformed model, accounting for BCF\nL545@P15: is:\nL546@P15: 𝑆𝑆 = 1.865 × 𝑇𝑇𝑇 0.933 × 𝑄0.0431.\nL547@P15: Previous models\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSurrogate memo v2.doc.docx (https://water.usgs.gov/admin/memo/SW/sw.2016.07%2Bwq.2016.10.pdf)\n【turn308view2】 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 40; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn296view1\",\"lineno\":618}); Total lines: 1021\nL587@P19: logTURB -0.6600 1.000 -0.7150\nL588@P19: logQ -0.0075 -0.715 1.0000\nL589@P19: Outlier Test Criteria\nL590@P19: Leverage Cook's D DFFITS\nL591@P19:  0.050 0.194 0.258\nL592@P19: Flagged Observations\nL593@P19:  logSSC Estimate Residual Standard Residual Studentized Residual Leverage Cook's D DFFITS\nL594@P19: 6/9/2005 10:55 2.670 2.840 -0.1740 -2.090 -2.120 0.03630 0.05470 -0.411\nL595@P19: 5/8/2007 11:30 2.440 2.580 -0.1410 -1.690 -1.710 0.03600 0.03560 -0.329\nL596@P19: 5/25/2007 13:10 2.450 2.600 -0.1530 -1.830 -1.850 0.03820 0.04460 -0.369\nL597@P19: 8/5/2008 9:30 1.910 1.700 0.2130 2.530 2.590 0.01390 0.03000 0.307\nL598@P19: 4/6/2009 13:05 2.220 1.970 0.2490 2.940 3.050 0.00893 0.02600 0.289\nL599@P19: 5/8/2009 12:55 3.190 3.040 0.1540 1.850 1.860 0.03220 0.03770 0.340\nL600@P19: 9/24/2009 11:20 2.930 3.070 -0.1420 -1.730 -1.740 0.05320 0.05580 -0.413\nL60